win32u: keep a layered window's pixels across a resize, hold it back until drawing reaches the new edges

Photoshop resizes a floating panel, a layered window, with SWP_NOCOPYBITS and redraws it through its own
queue instead of WM_PAINT. The new surface kept nothing, and nothing in the update region said a paint was
coming, so the resize hold did not apply and dark grey blocks went on screen.

The old pixels are now copied to the new surface for a visible layered top-level with no valid_rects, the
invalid area included: all of a window's own pixels are invalid after such a resize, so only its children
would be kept and the title row and edges stay dark.

A layered window that grew is then held back until drawing reaches its new edges, for at most 34 ms from the
first step of a drag. Photoshop sets a panel's width and height in two calls, so the edges add up. A new
shape marks the whole surface dirty, which reads as drawing, so under this hold that waits for the end of it.

Kill switches NEUTRON_LAYERED_KEEP_BITS=0 and NEUTRON_RESIZE_HOLD_EDGES=0.

--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -778,6 +778,7 @@
 
 static BOOL neutron_dc_suppress_flush(void);
 static BOOL neutron_resize_hold_active( struct window_surface *surface );
+static BOOL neutron_resize_hold_edges_waiting( struct window_surface *surface );
 
 void window_surface_flush( struct window_surface *surface )
 {
@@ -1074,13 +1075,14 @@
